> It is indeed, unless you want to eliminate the future tense.  Since we are
> not omniscient, especially about the future, any statement asbout the future
> is either "subjective" or "intentional".

I thought {ba} was objective. If I say {mi ba klama} and I never go, then
the statement was (is) false. If it is an intentional statement then it
should be {ai mi ba klama}, if it is subjective, then for instance {mi capu'o
klama}, which doesn't claim anything about the future.

Making {ba} objective doesn't mean eliminating the future tense. It means that
statements with {ba} are predictions. If they don't eventually realize, then
those predictions were false.

